    {
      "key": "boss",
      "title": "The helipad prep room",
      "debrief": "The twist, and the real lesson. Everyone flew to the high-chloride lake, but a still-transmitting beacon meant the pilot was alive — so must be somewhere milder. You plotted a different variable, water temperature, and read the gentlest lake instead. Choosing which column to look at is the whole game: the right plot of the right variable pointed you to Lava Lake while the crowd searched the wrong place.",
      "technique": "filter()/sort on a different variable — resist the obvious answer",
      "puzzleType": 2,
      "isBoss": true,
      "built": true,
      "authoring": {
        "tag": "boss",
        "scenePrompt": "This is a panorama. A 360-degree view from the centre of the helipad preparation room of a rustic Alaskan field station at night, the room wrapping continuously all the way around so the left and right edges meet seamlessly. Ahead, a workbench with an open laptop showing a faint water-temperature scatter-plot. To one side, a rack of glowing beacon-monitor screens each showing a different faint pulsing signal, an open equipment manual on the bench beneath them, and a dispatch clipboard ruled into a grid of empty squares hanging beside the rack. Opposite, a row of hanging insulated flight and survival suits with helmets on a shelf. Set into one wall, a large window onto a floodlit snowy helipad where a single helicopter waits, dark mountains beyond, and beside it a CLOSED exterior door — the way out. On another wall, an OPEN doorway giving back toward a dark, deserted radio and communications room of a rustic Alaskan field station at night that has an overturned chair in it. Around the rest of the room, steel lockers and cold instrument panels. Tense and quiet, warm amber instrument light against cold blue night, muted teal-and-amber palette, drifting haze, film grain, richly detailed. No people, no lettering, no captions, no text.",
        "doorPrompt": "The closed exterior door by the window now stands wide open to a blast of snow and floodlight, the waiting helicopter's rotors beginning to turn; the other, open doorway back toward the dark, deserted, and upended radio room is unchanged. Same painterly teal-and-amber style, no people, no lettering, no text."
